[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This kinetic sculpture uses two rotating discs&nbsp; that overlap and by varying the speed of the&nbsp;&nbsp; rotation or the design of the discs, you&nbsp; can get some different interesting visuals.&nbsp;&nbsp; Similar sculptures like this have been around for&nbsp; a while, so I thought surely there\'s going to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bunch of 3D printable models that I can&nbsp; just download, print, and build, right? So, I went looking and I found some&nbsp; with DC motors, some wind-powered,&nbsp;&nbsp; some like fidget spinners, and various&nbsp; designs with spring-loaded mechanisms.'

[00:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But, I was imagining something I can just&nbsp; put on a wall and turn on. No winding, no&nbsp;&nbsp; noisy mechanisms, something I&nbsp; can just place there, turn on,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'leave running. But, then I found this instructable&nbsp; by Dushyant Ahuja. It describes a printable design&nbsp;&nbsp; with two DC motors. However, it is also pretty&nbsp; big and I was imagining something more compact.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'He even mentions some future plans with a lot of&nbsp; good ideas and recommendations for improvements,&nbsp;&nbsp; but this was about eight years&nbsp; ago and I don\'t see any updates.'

[01:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Hi everyone, I\'m Rob and after&nbsp; seeing this instructable,&nbsp;&nbsp; I knew I had to build my own&nbsp; version and put my own spin on it. I started with a circular base and two&nbsp; motors pointing in the same direction&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'simply because I wanted the 3D printed gear&nbsp; transmissions to be hidden in the back. And&nbsp;&nbsp; the rotation is then transferred through these two&nbsp; nested shafts onto the interfaces in the front. And it works. The mechanism is hidden on one&nbsp; side, then it\'s being transferred through these&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nested shafts onto the front where&nbsp; I have these magnetic interfaces. So, I think the first version had a potential&nbsp; to work, but I didn\'t like that it was using DC&nbsp;&nbsp; motors. I decided that I will have more control&nbsp; using stepper motors just regarding the speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and I also didn\'t like it was using two different&nbsp; types of bearings. And I just knew I could make&nbsp;&nbsp; the thing more compact if I use the same bearings&nbsp; and if I just flip the motor around so I don\'t&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have them pointing in the same direction. So,&nbsp; that\'s what I did with the second version.&nbsp;&nbsp; One of the motors now faces forwards&nbsp; and one of them faces back as before,&nbsp;&nbsp; and the mechanism is using&nbsp; four of the same 608 bearings.'

[02:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And since I decided to use the&nbsp; stepper motors, that meant I had&nbsp;&nbsp; to fit the drivers and microcontroller&nbsp; and so on in there somewhere as well. I left this area here, which should be&nbsp; enough to fit all the components I need.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And based on that, I designed a custom PCB.&nbsp; But it was at this moment I knew I messed up. Look at this. Why is this mirrored?&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is what happens when you don\'t&nbsp; double-check the layouts you find online. Yeah, well, luckily this second version was okay.&nbsp;&nbsp; I\'m using the XIAO C6 TMC2209 drivers and&nbsp; I fitted DC buck converter in the back.'

[03:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, normally these cheap stepper motors are&nbsp; unipolar. That means I can\'t use the fancy drivers&nbsp;&nbsp; to make them go quietly. There is a modification,&nbsp; however, I can use. If you take the cover off and&nbsp;&nbsp; then cut the trace right here in the middle, you&nbsp; basically convert it from unipolar to bipolar.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But it\'s a bit of a hassle and it\'s hard to put&nbsp; the cover back on. So, I made this tool to make&nbsp;&nbsp; the modifications easier. All you need is a 2.5&nbsp; mm drill. First, you put it here to measure the&nbsp;&nbsp; distance you need, and then you use the second&nbsp; hole to drill into the motor, and you\'re done.'

[03:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And I also made a Gridfinity&nbsp; compatible version if you\'re into that.&nbsp;&nbsp; So, let\'s put it all together.&nbsp; We\'ve got the base and the PCB.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'One of the magnetic interfaces is also a gear&nbsp; and uses two bearings to fit on the shaft. Now, let\'s put the motors in one from each side,&nbsp; but with both cables facing the same direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, the shaft goes in with some spacers and&nbsp; again, it uses two bearings to fit in the base.&nbsp;&nbsp; The shaft is terminated on one side&nbsp; with the gear and on the other side&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with the magnetic interface and the whole&nbsp; thing holds together with an M4 bolt. I didn\'t want to mess around with shortening&nbsp; the cables on the motors, so I tried to fit them&nbsp;&nbsp; inside somehow. I used three zip ties to guide&nbsp; the cable around and it seems to fit pretty well.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, both cables go around in opposite&nbsp; directions and then I just tighten it.&nbsp;&nbsp; And with that, it\'s ready for a spin.'

[04:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'With the stepper motors and the drivers,&nbsp; it runs quietly and I have good control of&nbsp;&nbsp; the speed and with the motors facing in opposite&nbsp; directions, the whole thing is a lot more compact.&nbsp;&nbsp; The interface now uses front-facing&nbsp; magnets and these mechanical detents to&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hold the disc in place and&nbsp; prevent it from rotating. Quick note here about the drivers, they\'re quite&nbsp; powerful, so you can decrease the current by&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'setting this trimmer and the reference&nbsp; pin should have about 0.2 volts on it.&nbsp;&nbsp; To finish it, I\'m gluing an&nbsp; LED strip to the back cover,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'plugging it into the PCB and then&nbsp; adding covers from both sides. So, I\'m at a point where I think I have the&nbsp; mechanism somewhat figured out. I\'ve got this test&nbsp;&nbsp; disk here, but I need to figure out what design&nbsp; I want for those disks. So, there\'s a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'possible designs out there, and I\'m going to have&nbsp; to make models, at least a few models out of them&nbsp;&nbsp; to create different animations, right? So, to help&nbsp; me do that, I vibecoded this simulator where I can&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'draw something on this canvas on the left, it gets&nbsp; repeated in a pattern, and then I can animate it&nbsp;&nbsp; and see what sort of animations we get. And I&nbsp; can change the number of instances, right? You&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can see this wedge on the left here is getting&nbsp; bigger as I decrease the number of instances.&nbsp;&nbsp; So, let\'s try five. And anything I draw here&nbsp; is going to get repeated and drawn on the right&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'canvas. So, let\'s let\'s do some experiments&nbsp; with this. And after playing around with this&nbsp;&nbsp; tool a little bit, I had a few designs that&nbsp; I liked. If you\'d like to try the simulator,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I will leave a link in the description of this&nbsp; video. And these are the designs I came up with. So, okay, I had some fun with the designs,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but I had to model them somehow&nbsp; now and fit them onto the printers. To do that, I had a model in CAD with the proper&nbsp; interface in the middle. So, I exported that as an&nbsp;&nbsp; SVG, imported that to Inkscape, and basically&nbsp; used it as a canvas to replicate the design.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And then I cut it so I had only a quarter left.&nbsp; And by reducing the size into 1/4, I can finally&nbsp;&nbsp; print that. Luckily, I can just import the SVG&nbsp; file directly from Inkscape to PrusaSlicer and&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'print it. So, I have the four quarters that are&nbsp; connected in the middle by these interfaces.&nbsp;&nbsp; And those have magnets glued inside.&nbsp; And everything should just fit together.'

[07:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, that was the magnetic interface in the&nbsp; middle of the disc, but I also added these&nbsp;&nbsp; small parts around the circumference of the&nbsp; disc to better hold the four quarters together.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And I\'m basically just gluing them&nbsp; in from both sides of the disc. And with the two discs complete, it&nbsp; was finally time to see it in action.'

[08:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music]'

[08:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Okay, that seems to be working pretty&nbsp; well, but the main reason why I made&nbsp;&nbsp; the interfaces magnetic is so that I can&nbsp; quickly change into a different set of discs.&nbsp;&nbsp; So, it was back to Inkscape, Prusa Slicer, and&nbsp; well, the assembly is pretty much the same. And maybe more discs. Again, Inkscape assembly,&nbsp; Inkscape assembly. Well, you get the idea.'

[09:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Okay, I think this might be enough discs now. [music]'

[10:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The microcontroller I used has Wi-Fi, which gives&nbsp; me the opportunity to make a remote controller,&nbsp;&nbsp; which I did. Two potentiometers for&nbsp; control, a button to change the modes,&nbsp;&nbsp; and a light to see which mode you\'re in.&nbsp; And that goes into a little box. I could&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'put a battery in there and make it completely&nbsp; wireless, but I thought USB power is good enough. I\'m using it in three modes. The first one just&nbsp; controls the speed of each disk. So, the left knob&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'controls one and the right knob controls the speed&nbsp; of the second disk. Then, by hitting the button,&nbsp;&nbsp; I switch into color mode, and one knob controls&nbsp; the color, the other controls the spread of the&nbsp;&nbsp; colors, the gradient. And in the third mode, the&nbsp; speeds of the disks automatically sweep through&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different speeds, so they slow down and speed&nbsp; up. And here is where the problems begin. It&nbsp;&nbsp; doesn\'t really handle well any speed changes, but&nbsp; it works well enough that I\'m okay with it. And&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'also, the video is getting quite long, so I&nbsp; don\'t think I\'m going to fix it right now. Overall, I\'m pretty happy with how it turned&nbsp; out. It has its limitations, especially when&nbsp;&nbsp; changing speeds when using the controller, and at&nbsp; first I thought I would be able to just have it&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'automatically sweep through slow and fast speeds,&nbsp; but it doesn\'t handle speed changes very well.&nbsp;&nbsp; I think it\'s because of the play in the mechanism.&nbsp; So, the stepper motors have 1 to 64 reduction if&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m not mistaken already inside of them, and then&nbsp; I\'m using 3D printed gears to get the rotation&nbsp;&nbsp; from the motor shafts onto this main shaft of&nbsp; the mechanism. So, I think that introduces some&nbsp;&nbsp; backlash as well, and the backlash then produces&nbsp; the inconsistencies in speed, I think. It might&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'be something else. But, other than that, I think&nbsp; it\'s pretty cool, and who knows? Maybe in another&nbsp;&nbsp; 8 years somebody else will pick up the project&nbsp; and make it better again. Maybe it will be me,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'maybe not. I have other plans for projects&nbsp; right now. Until then, keep building cool stuff.'
